      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Buying a treadmill for an apartment is not mainly a noise problem, though that is the
          part everyone worries about. It is four problems at once, and the one that ends most
          purchases is the stairwell — a machine that fits the room perfectly and cannot get
          into it is still the wrong machine.
        </p>

        <div id="constraints" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Four Constraints, In the Order They Bite</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The stairwell and the door.</strong> This is the constraint that most often
            ends a purchase, and it is the one people check last. Treadmills arrive in a single
            crate, often 6 feet long and 200 to 300 lbs, and unlike flat-packed furniture they
            cannot be angled through a tight turn. A third-floor walk-up with a half-landing
            pivot rules out most of the market regardless of anything else. Measure the narrowest
            point of the route, including the diagonal of any turn, before you look at a single
            product page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The neighbour below.</strong> Treadmill noise splits into two kinds and they
            behave completely differently. Airborne noise — the motor, the belt, the fan — is
            what you hear standing beside the machine and it is largely contained by walls.
            Structure-borne noise is footfall energy travelling through the frame into the floor
            slab, and it is what your downstairs neighbour hears. Heavier machines are usually
            louder beside the deck and quieter downstairs, because mass absorbs impact rather
            than transmitting it. That inversion catches people out constantly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floor space, folded and unfolded.</strong> An apartment treadmill usually has
            to disappear between sessions. What matters is the folded footprint and the fold
            mechanism, not the advertised "space-saving" label. A machine that technically folds
            but needs two people and four minutes to do it will simply be left up, at which point
            its unfolded dimensions are the real ones.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The lease.</strong> Many leases contain clauses about exercise equipment,
            floor loading or noise hours, and some buildings prohibit treadmills outright. This
            costs nothing to check and occasionally saves a great deal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-truth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What the Neighbour Below Actually Hears</h2>
<p>
            The single most useful thing to understand here is that the sound you hear and the
            sound they hear are produced by different mechanisms and rank machines differently.
            Rank the same machines by what you hear beside the deck and by what reaches the room
            below and the two lists need not match — a machine that is quiet at the console can be
            unremarkable downstairs, and a louder, heavier one can be among the better ones below.
          </p>
          <p>
            The reason is mass and isolation. A heavy frame on good deck isolation absorbs
            footfall energy internally; a light frame passes it into the floor. Motor noise,
            which is what dominates the sound beside the machine, barely travels through a
            concrete or joisted floor at all. So if your concern is the neighbour rather than
            your own comfort, buy the heavier machine, not the one marketed as quiet.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three interventions genuinely help, in descending order of effect. A proper rubber
            <a href="/do-you-need-a-treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill mat</a>, at least 3/4 inch thick, decouples the frame from the floor and is by
            some distance the most effective thing you can buy. Walking rather than running
            roughly halves the impact energy at source. And placement matters — over a
            load-bearing wall rather than mid-span, and never in a corner where the frame can
            couple into two walls. Our
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmill guide</a>
            explains all three in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose for Apartment Use</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines, and nothing on this page should be read as if we
            had. They were ranked from the manufacturers' published specifications against the
            four apartment constraints: assembled weight against a typical apartment delivery
            route, including a 32-inch doorway and a half-landing turn; the fold mechanism,
            because one person folding alone is the realistic case in an apartment; the folded
            footprint; and frame mass, as the best available guide to how much footfall energy
            reaches the floor below.
          </p>
          <p>
            The most important point is not about any machine. Structure-borne noise depends
            heavily on what sits between the frame and the floor, and a 3/4-inch rubber mat that
            decouples the two typically does more for the room below than the choice between one
            machine and another. Put plainly: the mat may matter more than the machine. If you
            take one thing from this page, take that.
          </p>
          <p>
            The fold mechanism is the second. A machine that folds on paper but takes real effort
            with one person is likely to be left up, and in an apartment that is the difference
            between a machine that gets put away and a machine that becomes furniture. Hydraulic
            and assisted folds are the ones to prefer.
          </p>
        </div>

